   170	## 3. Jelonek--Chau degree bounds
   171	
   172	Write `M=deg P >= E=deg Q` in the same target coordinates used for
   173	`(d,n)`, and keep `N=4` separate from `M`.
   174	
   175	### 3.1 Jelonek: a coordinate-degree bound, not an `N`-bound
   176	
   177	Jelonek's 1993 Theorem 15 says for a dominant
   178	`f=(f_1,...,f_m):C^m -> C^m`
   179	
   180	```text
   181	deg S_f <= (product_i deg f_i - mu(f))/min_i deg f_i.
   182	```
   183	
   184	Here `mu(f)` is geometric degree.  In this lane it gives
   185	
   186	```text
   187	deg A_F <= (M E-4)/E = M-4/E < M.
   188	```
   189	
   190	Since `A_F=D_1 union D_0` is reduced with distinct components,
   191	`deg A_F=d+deg D_0`; integrality therefore yields the useful but
   192	coordinate-dependent estimate
   193	
   194	```text
   195	d+deg D_0 <= M-1,             hence d <= M-2.                 (3.1)
   196	```
   197	
   198	The later quantitative theorem of Jelonek--Lason says that the
   199	nonproperness set of a polynomial map of algebraic degree `M` is covered by
   200	parametric curves of degree at most `M-1` (Theorem 3.2); this again uses
   201	**algebraic** degree, not geometric degree.  The 1999 *Testing sets for
   202	properness* paper supplies testing-set and uniruledness theorems, but no
   203	replacement of `M` by `mu(f)` in (3.1).  In particular Bezout gives only
   204	`4<=ME`, the wrong direction.  Proposition 2.1 also explicitly makes `M`
   205	unbounded under target postcomposition while `N=4` stays fixed.  Thus none
   206	of these results produces a numerical `B(N)`.
   207	
   208	### 3.2 Chau: a genuine constraint on the reduced pair
   209	
   210	Chau 1999, Theorem 4.4(E1), applies after a generic source linear change
   211	(making the coordinates monic in `y`) and says that every dicritical-image
   212	parametrisation `(P_phi,Q_phi)` satisfies
   213	
   214	```text
   215	deg P_phi / deg Q_phi = deg P / deg Q.                         (3.2)
   216	```
   217	
   218	Because `s_1=s_0=1`, these are birational parametrisations of `D_1,D_0`.
   219	Put
   220	
   221	```text
   222	g=gcd(d,n),       (u,v)=(d/g,n/g),
   223	k=gcd(M,E),       (M,E)=k(u,v).
   224	```
   225	
   226	Chau's Theorem B says that, if `v>1`, there are nonnegative integers
   227	`r,s`, not both zero, such that
   228	
   229	```text
   230	N = r u+s v >= min(2v,u);                                     (3.3)
   231	```
   232	
   233	the alternative in the theorem is `v=1`.  At `N=4`, (3.3) is elementary
   234	to solve.  If `v>=3`, the lower bound forces `u<=4`, hence coprimality and
   235	`u>v` give only `(u,v)=(4,3)`.  If `v=2`, coprimality makes `u` any odd
   236	integer at least three, and `4=0*u+2*2` is not excluded.  Therefore
