   335	
   336	## 5. The \(S_4\)-cover and Euler characteristics
   337	
   338	Let `r=#Sing D_1`.  Normalisation by `A^1` and identification of two
   339	points at each double point give
   340	
   341	```text
   342	chi_c(D_1)=1-r,       chi_c(D_1-Sing D_1)=1-2r,
   343	chi_c(A^2-D_1)=r.
   344	```
   345	
   346	The fibre cardinalities of `q:Y->A^2` are respectively four, three, and
   347	two on the complement, smooth branch locus, and singular locus (the last
   348	from two disjoint transpositions).  Therefore
   349	
   350	```text
   351	chi_c(Y)=4r+3(1-2r)+2r=3.                                    (5.1)
   352	```
   353	
   354	This is an identity independent of `r,d,n`; the unbranched component
   355	`D_0` cannot change it.
   356	
   357	There is, however, an exact way to expose the missing degree invariant.
   358	Choose a generic affine target line `L`, avoiding all special points and
   359	with a different point at infinity from the unique one of `D_1`.  It meets
   360	`D_1` transversely in `d` points.  The generic-line theorem for curve
   361	complements makes
   362	`pi_1(L-D_1)->pi_1(A^2-D_1)` surjective, so the restricted degree-four
   363	cover `C_L=q^{-1}(L)` is connected.  Let `Pi in S_4` be the ordered product
   364	of its `d` transposition monodromies, and let `c(Pi)` be its number of
   365	cycles.  On compactifying the cover, finite ramification contributes `d`
   366	and ramification over infinity contributes `4-c(Pi)`.  Riemann--Hurwitz
   367	gives
   368	
   369	```text
