    55	cardinalities, `a_D`, `a_p`, monodromy classes, normalisations, and incidences
    56	are all pulled through the target isomorphism. Thus `Br=D_1`, `D_0`
    57	unbranched, transposition meridians, disjoint local transpositions at
    58	`Sing D_1`, and the two-component residual structure are preserved.
    59	
    60	For `gamma(t)=(p(t),q(t))` birational on `D_1`, choose `k` with
    61	`k deg p > deg q`. Then `T_k gamma=(p,q+p^k)` has coordinate degrees
    62	`(deg p,k deg p)`, with no top-degree cancellation. Since the map remains
    63	birational and the projective pullback of a generic line has degree
    64	`k deg p`, the ordinary plane degree of `T_k(D_1)` is `k deg p`, unbounded.
    65	
    66	The literal statement "no finite raw-degree bound exists" is only meaningful
    67	after excluding the vacuous case. What is proved is: if the residual class is
    68	nonempty, its target-automorphism orbit has unbounded ordinary `deg D_1`.
    69	Therefore any promoted payoff must be target-invariant, or must first fix a
    70	canonical target gauge.
    71	
    72	## Item 2: Jelonek/Chau Typing
    73	
    74	Verdict: **PASS.**
    75	
    76	Jelonek 1993 Theorem 15 bounds the hypersurface of nonproperness by the
    77	coordinate degrees:
    78	
    79	```text
    80	deg S_f <= ((prod_i deg f_i) - mu(f)) / min_i deg f_i.
    81	```
    82	
    83	For `F=(P,Q)`, `deg P=M >= E=deg Q`, and `mu(F)=4`, this gives
    84	`deg A_F <= (ME-4)/E < M`, hence, integrally, `deg A_F <= M-1`.
    85	Since `A_F=D_1 union D_0` is reduced with distinct curve components,
    86	`d+deg D_0 <= M-1`, so `d <= M-2`. This is a coordinate-degree estimate,
    87	not an `N=4` estimate. Jelonek-Lason Theorem 3.2 likewise uses the
    88	algebraic degree `d` of the map and covers `S_f` by parametric curves of
    89	degree at most `d-1`; it does not replace algebraic degree by geometric
    90	degree. The "Testing sets" PDF was not available as primary bytes here and
    91	is not needed: the hashed Jelonek/Jelonek-Lason statements already show the
    92	claimed non-bound.
    93	
    94	Chau 1999 applies to a nonzero constant-Jacobian map after a generic source
    95	linear change making the polynomials monic in `y`. Theorem 4.4(E1) gives, for
    96	each dicritical-image parametrisation, the same degree ratio as
    97	`deg P / deg Q`. Because the residual has `s_1=s_0=1`, the parametrisation
    98	degree pair of each component is a scalar multiple of the reduced target
    99	degree pair. Write
   100	
   101	```text
   102	g=gcd(d,n),       (u,v)=(d/g,n/g),
   103	gcd(deg P,deg Q)=k,       (deg P,deg Q)=k(u,v).
   104	```
   105	
   106	Chau Theorem B says either `v=1`, or
   107	`4 = r u + s v >= min(2v,u)` with `r,s >= 0`, not both zero. For `v=2`,
   108	coprimality gives `u` odd and `u>=3`, and `4=2*2` is allowed. For `v>=3`,
   109	`min(2v,u)<=4` forces `u<=4`; with `u>v` and coprime this leaves only
   110	`(u,v)=(4,3)`. Thus the charged trichotomy
   111	
   112	```text
   113	(u,1),       (odd u,2),       (4,3)
   114	```
   115	
   116	is exactly the `N=4` consequence. Finally, if `D_0` has scale `g_0` on the
   117	same reduced pair, Jelonek gives `(g+g_0)u <= ku-1`; since `g_0>=1`, this
   118	implies `g <= k-2 = gcd(deg P,deg Q)-2`. This still does not bound `g`
   119	unless a separate bound on the target coordinate degrees is supplied.
   120	
   121	## Item 3: The Identity d = 2g_L + c(Pi) + 2
   122	
   123	Verdict: **PASS as an identity, FAIL as a degree bound.**
   124	
   125	Here `g_L` must mean the genus of the compact normalization of the finite
   126	degree-four curve `q^{-1}(L) -> L`, after compactifying a generic affine target
   127	line `L` to `P^1`. Choose `L` avoiding `Sing D_1`, meeting `D_1` transversely
   128	in `d` points, and generic enough that the line-complement map on
   129	fundamental groups is surjective. Since the global degree-four cover is
   130	connected, the restricted cover over `L-D_1` is connected.
   131	
   132	Each of the `d` finite branch points has transposition monodromy, so its
   133	ramification contribution is `1`. Let `Pi` be the product of the finite
   134	transpositions; the monodromy over infinity is `Pi^{-1}`, and if `c(Pi)` is
   135	the number of cycles on four letters, the ramification contribution over
   136	infinity is `4-c(Pi)`. Riemann-Hurwitz for a degree-four cover of `P^1` gives
   137	
   138	```text
   139	2g_L - 2 = -8 + d + 4 - c(Pi),
   140	so d = 2g_L + c(Pi) + 2.
   141	```
   142	
   143	Equivalently, the affine curve `q^{-1}(L)` has
   144	`chi_c = 4(1-d)+3d = 4-d`, while its compactification has `c(Pi)` punctures,
   145	again giving `d=2g_L+c(Pi)+2`. The derivation is sound, but it merely converts
   146	raw degree into compact slice genus plus the infinity permutation. No promoted
   147	input bounds `g_L`.
   148	
   149	## Item 4: Noncoprime List Through d <= 9
   150	
   151	Verdict: **PASS.**
   152	
   153	Use only `d>n>=1`, `g=gcd(d,n)>=2`, and the Chau shapes for
   154	`(u,v)=(d/g,n/g)`.
   155	
   156	The family `(u,1)` is exactly `n=g` with `n|d` and `d/n=u>=2`. Through
   157	`d<=9` this gives
   158	
   159	```text
   160	(4,2);
   161	(6,2),(6,3);
   162	(8,2),(8,4);
   163	(9,3).
   164	```
   165	
   166	The family `(odd u,2)` gives `d=gu`, `n=2g`, with odd `u>=3`. Through
   167	`d<=9` this contributes
   168	
   169	```text
   170	(6,4), (9,6).
   171	```
   172	
   173	The family `(4,3)` gives only `g=2` in this range:
   174	
   175	```text
   176	(8,6).
   177	```
   178	
   179	Combining and ordering by `d` yields exactly
   180	
   181	```text
   182	(4,2);
   183	(6,2),(6,3),(6,4);
   184	(8,2),(8,4),(8,6);
   185	(9,3),(9,6).
   186	```
   187	
   188	The identity `d=2g_L+c(Pi)+2` imposes no additional pair-level deletion in
   189	this range: for the listed even `d`, an even infinity permutation with
   190	`c=2` or `4` is the only parity issue, and for `d=9`, `c=1` or `3` is
   191	compatible with an integral nonnegative genus. The scale inequality
   192	`g<=gcd(deg P,deg Q)-2` also gives no finite deletion without a bound on the
   193	target degree gcd.
   194	
   195	## Item 5: Coprime Kill-List Reduction
   196	
   197	Verdict: **PASS, but only as an intermediate-table filter.**
   198	
   199	The promoted coordinator table lists the `d<=9` survivors under `(C1)+A(3)`:
   200	
   201	```text
   202	(4,3),(5,4),(7,4),(8,3),(9,4),(9,8).
   203	```
   204	
   205	Because these are coprime, each pair is already its own reduced Chau pair
   206	`(u,v)`. Chau's `N=4` trichotomy keeps `(4,3)` and deletes the other five:
   207	
   208	```text
   209	(5,4): min(8,5)=5 > 4;
   210	(7,4): min(8,7)=7 > 4;
   211	(8,3): min(6,8)=6 > 4;
   212	(9,4): min(8,9)=8 > 4;
   213	(9,8): min(16,9)=9 > 4.
   214	```
   215	
   216	Thus the elimination is sourced by the hashed Chau Theorem B together with
   217	Theorem 4.4(E1)'s ratio identification for residual dicritical images. This
   218	does not mean `(4,3)` is promoted as an actual residual escape; it is just the
   219	only member of that intermediate `d<=9` coprime survivor table not removed by
   220	the Chau shape filter. The separately promoted PI1-S4 Main Theorem is stronger
   221	on its stated coprime scope.
   222	
   223	## Item 6: CLOSE-RESIDUAL Invariant Caveat
   224	
   225	Verdict: **PASS; the caveat is necessary.**
   226	
   227	The CLOSE-RESIDUAL payoff "`deg D_1<=4` closes the residual" is a statement
   228	about a chosen target embedding of the curve. Item 1 shows that the same
   229	Keller/residual object can be moved by target automorphisms to arbitrarily
   230	large raw degree without changing the cover problem. Therefore raw
   231	`deg D_1<=4` is not an invariant campaign target.
   232	
   233	The invariant formulation is sound:
   234	
   235	```text
   236	if min_{T in Aut(A^2)} deg T(D_1) <= 4, then apply CLOSE-RESIDUAL to T(D_1)
   237	and pull the contradiction back by T^{-1}.
   238	```
