The following result is usually known as Abhyankar-Moh (semigroup) Theorem. (See
[4, 3, 5, 18, 17] as references).
Theorem 2.1. Let C be a curve having only one place at infinity and assume that char(k)
does not divide gcd(−νC,p (x), −νC,p (y)). Then, there exists a finite sequence of positive
integers ∆ = {δ0 , δ1 , . . . , δg } that generates the semigroup SC,∞ and satisfies the following
conditions:
(1) If di = gcd(δ0 , δ1 , . . . , δi−1 ), for 1 ≤ i ≤ g + 1, and ni = di /di+1 , 1 ≤ i ≤ g, then
dg+1 = 1 and ni > 1 for 1 ≤ i ≤ g.
(2) For 1 ≤ i ≤ g, ni δi belongs to the subsemigroup of the non-negative integers, N,
generated by δ0 , δ1 , . . . , δi−1 , that we usually denote hδ0 , δ1 , . . . , δi−1 i.
(3) δ0 > δ1 and δi < δi−1 ni−1 for i = 2, 3, . . . , g.
Throughout this paper, a sequence ∆ = {δ0 , δ1 , . . . , δg } of positive integers that satisfies
the conditions (1), (2) and (3) of Theorem 2.1 will be named a δ-sequence in N>0 , where
N>0 denotes the set of strictly positive integers.
Remark 2.1. The proof of Theorem 2.1 is based on the fact that, under the assumptions
of Definition 2.3 on the polynomial f (x, y) ∈ k[x, y] defining C, a specific family of approximates for the curve (called approximate roots) can be obtained. We point out here
that, given an equation f (x, y) = 0 for the curve C, the mentioned assumptions on f can
be attained by means of a change of variables, obtaining an isomorphic curve C ′ satisfying
all the requirements. Then, the generators of SC ′ ,∞ = SC,∞ mentioned in the statement
of Theorem 2.1 are just the values δ0 , . . . , δg associated with the family of approximates
given by the approximate roots of C ′ (see Definition 2.3). Notice also that δ0 coincides
with deg(C ′ ).
From Equality (2), Remark 2.1 and the properties that define the families of approximates, it is easy to deduce the following result, which shows how the maximal contact
values of the singularity at infinity of a curve C as in Theorem 2.1 (see [6, Ch. 4], for
instance) can be expressed in terms of a δ-sequence in N>0 attached to the semigroup
SC,∞ .
Proposition 2.1. Let C be a curve satisfying the hypotheses of Theorem 2.1 and let
{β̄i }gi=0 be the set of maximal contact values of the germ of C at p. Then, there exists a
δ-sequence in N>0 , {δi }si=0 , generating the semigroup SC,∞ , such that if νC,p (u) does not

